Examine src/test/input/ocaml-stdlib. Look at the README. See how it lists some modules in the OCaml stdlib, as well as names similar to filenames of existing files? Now look at the other files in there, specifically the ones that end in the *.types file extension. See how for each one, it has a list of type declarations? Look specifically at the ones that end in *-mod.types. The "mod" substring in the filename means it describes a module in the OCaml stdlib. The number at the beginning is a unique identifier number for the file. I haven't created *-mod.types files for all the modules in the Stdlib yet. The ones at the bottom of the README, on each line that begins with module, are the ones that are remaining.

Here is what you should do. For each module in README, find all the type signatures for the functions in the module. You can do this by somehow looking in the stdlib source code online, or you can choose to navigate to https://ocaml.org/manual/5.4/api/Stdlib.MODULENAMEHERE.html and read the page. On the page, there are many function signature declarations that begin with the keyword "val". Those are the most important ones. For each module, make a new NUMBER-NAME-mod.types file. Pick a reasonable unique number, and a reasonable unique name that is short. Take all of those signature declarations in that module and place them in the file with the same formatting as I've done so far. Notice that the README, there are notes that link filenames with brief descriptions, as well as dependencies on other type declarations that are outside the module. They are separated by pipes |. For each module, you should add a line in the README in an appropriate place linking your chosen filename to the name of the module. There are also type declarations that begin with the keyword "type". When you see those in a module, you must note it in a comment that begins with "//". Please do it in the README instead of the .types file, I did it in the .types files and that's a mistake.

